Z||000003||terryk||11-06-2000||07:10 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.obdii.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.obdii.com/</A> <P>You can learn more there, but based on your question, all OBD II PCM/ECMS/BCM (Powertrain, Engine, Body) Control Modules are flash programmable meaning the calibrations can only be changed by sending the new calibrations to the module directly (via a PC or special scanner like the Tech II) rather than removing a plug in prom (mem-cal/calibrator), making the changes and plug it back in.<P>So with that in mind, changing the calibrations or disabling error codes is more difficult. Rather than being able to go in and disable for instances, the vehicle anti-theft or shift solenoid errors, you have to fake the PCM/ECM/BCM into thinking that stuff is there. This makes for some pretty kludgey engine swaps.<P>The actually difference between the two are that OBDI was the beginning of trying to predict future emissions failures. OBDII is the refined version of that. <P>Many OBDI PCM/ECM/BCM's are not flash (they have the removable calibrator). At the same time, if no one knows what to change to clear up the errors or disable certain circuits, then either are as good or bad depending on your point of view.<P>Tons of people will claim someone is working on the one you are interested in. Only a few have been hacked for commerical changing and the cost is high ($250+). The F and Y body's have been worked out pretty well. The Buick SC II's have been hacked, but few people with reprogram them. <P>If you move the engine and trans together, OBDII is not much of a problem for the most part although later cars might actually look for other modules on the bus (HVAC/ABS/Traction Control, etc).<P>I think that all of this is what makes people shy away from OBDII swaps.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||11-07-2000||01:32 AM||||OBD I started in 1978 and OBD II started in 1994. OBD III started in 1999 but only in Cadillac's. Some new cars have OBD III but not all. These years are for cars. I think trucks are different.||||reg||1
